            "x": 0,
            "y": 72
          },
          "options": {
            "mode": "markdown",
            "content": "\n## What this dashboard can and cannot tell you\n\nEverything here is measured **at the broker**. That is enough to answer \"is my\ntopic healthy\" and \"is my consumer keeping up\", and it is *not* enough to answer\n\"why is my producer slow\". Specifically, the broker cannot see:\n\n- producer retry rate, batch size, buffer exhaustion, or request latency as the\n  client experienced it\n- consumer poll duration, processing time, or `records-lag-max` from the client's\n  own view\n- rebalance counts and durations (member-count dips here are a proxy, not the truth)\n- end-to-end latency from produce to consume\n\nThose require scraping the **client applications** (JMX exporter sidecar or\nMicrometer on the app itself). Adding that is the single biggest upgrade available\nto this dashboard.\n\n## Derivations worth knowing about\n\n| Number | How it is computed | Caveat |\n|---|---|---|\n| Produce rate | `rate()` over log-end-offset | Counts transaction markers on transactional topics; slightly over-reports |\n| Seconds behind | lag \u00f7 produce rate | If production stops, this inflates \u2014 by design, since the data is genuinely getting older |\n| Retention achieved | (end offset \u2212 start offset) \u00f7 produce rate | Assumes the current rate held historically; approximate after a traffic spike |\n| Since last produced | subquery over a 6h window | Shows \"> 6h\" rather than a number once traffic has been absent longer than that |\n| Consumer members | `kafka_consumergroup_members` | Reported per group, not per topic \u2014 a group consuming several topics shows its total |\n\n## Sampling\n\n`kafka_exporter` polls the cluster on each scrape, so lag is a **snapshot**, not a\ncontinuous measurement. At 30s scrape intervals and high throughput, lag charts\nlook spiky; that is the sampling, not your consumer. Do not alert on a single\nsample \u2014 always use `avg_over_time` or `for:` in the alert rule.\n"
          },
          "transparent": false
        }
